  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1240 AM EST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nA cold front has moved east of the area, and high pressure will be \nbuilding in from the northwest through today. Clouds in the eastern \nhalf of the area have been slow to erode, and probably won't clear \ncompletely until after daytime boundary layer mixing kicks in. A \nstrengthening pressure gradient ahead of the building high will lead \nto some gusty NE winds through the TN Valley this afternoon, mainly \nbetween 20-30 mph.\n\nA double-barrel trough will be taking shape over the Plains tonight, \nwith a vort max dropping SE out of central Canada to the upper \nMidwest, and a second southern stream vort max over TX/AR/LA. These \ntwo disturbances merge over the Ozarks/MO Valley late Wednesday, \nbecoming a negatively-tilted closed low that tracks across the OH/TN \nValley region on Thursday. Surface cyclogenesis ensues off the GA/SC \ncoast Thursday morning, transporting deep moisture from the Atlantic \ntoward the Appalachians, over a CAD wedge. The 850 mb easterly flow \npicks up to 40-50 kt starting Wednesday afternoon and continuing \ninto Thursday morning. While the low position and 850 mb wind \ndirection is not ideal for a mountain wave wind event (east, rather \nthan southeast), we could still see gusty winds in the mountains,\nmaybe up to 50 mph. So far, this looks like an Advisory level \nwind event for the mountains. Gusty winds develop area-wide \nThursday afternoon as the departing low continues to intensify in \nthe DelMarVa region and winds shift to a SW direction up the TN \nValley. \n\nRain amounts for this event appear to have trended down slightly \nfrom previous model runs, and are now in the range of a half inch \nnorth to around 1 inch south. This will be beneficial for the dry\nconditions we have had recently. Temperatures aloft will drop \nsharply on Thursday evening as the upper low moves directly \noverhead, which may allow for a change to snow for northern \nportions of the area, with only light accumulations expected in \nthe mountains and higher elevations of SW VA. \n\nAn active pattern will bring another shortwave trough toward the \narea late Sunday, with rain chances Sunday night and Monday\nmorning.",
